{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-typescript/1794514249000-engineer-ts-callsite-adoption.qwen3.8-max.findings.md | sed -n '145,168p;389,419p;584,620p;945,1037p;1061,1158p'\"","aggregated_output":"   145\t| `test/yang/typescript/antlr/lower_test.clj` | **71-79** | The golden builder `cells` (used only by `var-declaration-golden-test`) wraps its result in `u/annotate-call-sites`. Repairs the three goldens at `:90` (`\"var x = 1;\"`), `:96` (`\"let y = 2;\"`), `:102` (`\"let z;\"`). |\n   146\t| | **140-145** | `literal-golden-test`'s BigInt expectation — the `js/string->bigint` application — wrapped in `u/annotate-call-sites`. |\n   147\t| `test/yang/typescript/antlr/lower_portable_test.cljc` | **206-217** | `hand-packet-golden-test`'s literal expected tree wrapped in `u/annotate-call-sites`. **This was the only portable breaking site**, i.e. it would have gone red on Node and Dart as well as the JVM. |\n   148\t| `test/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc` | **61-63** | `jssp-module-uast-is-one-wide-application-test`: `(dissoc (last operands) :tail?)` → `(dissoc (last operands) :tail? :yang/call-site)`, extending the file's own existing `:tail?`-stripping idiom rather than inventing a new one. Broken only because I marked `jssp` (J1). The sibling assertion at `:67-70` targets a `:literal` and needed no change. |\n   149\t\n   150\t**Repair idiom — CORRECTED in FIX-ROUND-2 (item 3a).** Five of the six repairs finalize\n   151\tthe *expected* side with the same generic walker (`lower_test.clj:71-79` and `:140-145`,\n   152\t`lower_portable_test.cljc:206-217`): `(u/annotate-call-sites <literal tree>)` keeps each\n   153\tgolden a literal, hand-written tree and additionally pins that the producer's output is\n   154\t*exactly* the shared walk's output over it, whereas a stripped comparison would stop\n   155\tpinning the marking at all.\n   156\t\n   157\t**The sixth does the opposite, and my original §1.2 wording was wrong to imply otherwise.**\n   158\t`linked_prelude_test.cljc:61-63` strips the marker from the **PRODUCED** side —\n   159\t`(dissoc (last operands) :tail? :yang/call-site)` removes `:yang/call-site` from\n   160\t`hooks/module-uast`'s delivered `require` operand before comparing it to\n   161\t`(u/sexp->uast '(require (quote js)))`. It is a *strip-the-actual* repair, not a\n   162\tfinalize-the-expected one, because that assertion's whole purpose is to compare one operand\n   163\tof the published module tree against a plain `sexp->uast` rendering; re-deriving the expected\n   164\tside through the finalizer there would have obscured what the assertion is about. sol\n   165\tverified that precision is preserved — the assertion still pins the operand's operator, its\n   166\tliteral `(quote js)` operand, and the absence of everything except the two normalization keys\n   167\t— so the choice stands; only my description of it was inaccurate. The repo's prior art for the\n   168\tstripping direction is the `(dissoc … :tail?)` idiom already on that very line, and the private\n   389\tIndependent corroboration that the claimed lane is already production-proven for a ts\n   390\tcomposition: `yang.core.call/module-uast` has been C1-marked since cc1\n   391\t(`yang/core/call.cljc:168-175`) and `linked_harness`'s `published` delay publishes it\n   392\t*first*, into the same store. This is the mechanism satisfying brief requirement 2, and\n   393\t`canonical-invariance-test` pins it.\n   394\t\n   395\tAlso verified unaffected: `core/free-name-vec` over the newly-marked `module-uast`\n   396\t(`yang/core/prelude.cljc:80-96` branches on `(:type node)` and returns `acc` for any\n   397\tnon-map value, so the marker's value `1` is a no-op and the key is never visited);\n   398\t`core/module-spec` (`:138-159`) is AST-shape-agnostic; `vm/ast->datoms` writes only\n   399\tgrammar fields.\n   400\t\n   401\t### 4.4 One real observable delta, decided consciously\n   402\t\n   403\t`safepoint/derive-envelope` on a ts envelope changes behaviour. Today `derive-call-sites`\n   404\t(`yang/safepoint.cljc:298-386`) finds no C1 rows and answers nil, so the derived envelope\n   405\tcarries `:yin/frontend-metadata []` and **no** `:yang.safepoint/correspondence`. After this\n   406\tchange it answers rows plus a correspondence. **No ts caller supplies `:injected-origin`** —\n   407\t`safepoint/transform` (`:455`) calls the 3-arity, as do\n   408\t`test/yang/typescript/antlr/e2e_test.clj:71-86`, `safepoint_test.clj:132-136`, and\n   409\t`linked_prelude_test.cljc:249-262` — so the injected hook applications stay **unclaimed by\n   410\tdesign** (`safepoint.cljc:303-306`: *\"without one it is recorded as injected and left\n   411\tunclaimed\"*) and the derived envelope's C1 rows deliberately do **not** cover the derived\n   412\ttree. That is safe today only because nothing re-projects a derived envelope: all four ts\n   413\tcall sites stop at `vm/semantic-bytecode->ast` / `tree-of` / `vm/validate-rows`, and\n   414\t`check-projected-claim!` fires only on `project-member`'s already-projected arm\n   415\t(`encoder.cljc:112-121`). **If any future ts host routes a derived envelope back through\n   416\t`encoder/project` or `encoder/load`, it will refuse\n   417\t`:yang/call-site-incomplete-coverage` until it supplies `:injected-origin`.** A latent trap,\n   418\tnot a present defect. See judgement call **J3**.\n   419\t\n   584\t**J3 — RULED by sol at step 7; no longer an open question. Now obligation `TS-SP-C1`.**\n   585\t*(This entry replaces my FIX-ROUND-1 \"ruling requested\" framing, which asked whether the ts\n   586\tsafepoint path should supply an admission origin. sol ruled; the text below is the ruling as I\n   587\tunderstand it and have implemented it.)*\n   588\t\n   589\tOmitting `:injected-origin` is a **NAMED OBLIGATION, `TS-SP-C1`**: a ts derived envelope\n   590\tcarries **partial correspondence**, and that is *not* complete derived-tree adoption. The\n   591\tinjected `js.sp/loop` / `jssp/loop` hook applications are recorded in\n   592\t`:yang.safepoint/correspondence` as `{:kind :safepoint/injected :path p}` with **no origin**,\n   593\twhile every surviving occurrence keeps its own incoming origin. Consequence, now a standing\n   594\trule rather than my judgement: **derived-envelope re-projection stays OUTSIDE the accepted C1\n   595\troutes until the shared-encoder gap is resolved.** The gap sol found is P1 and lives in the\n   596\tSHARED encoder at `yin/vm/encoder.cljc:115` (the already-projected member arm, which reads\n   597\t`:frontend-metadata` off the *member* — where `safepoint/derive-envelope` never puts it, since\n   598\tthe rows ride the *envelope*) and `:158` (`project-envelope`'s `mapcat` over members, which\n   599\tthen overwrites the envelope-level relation with the empty concatenation). Net effect:\n   600\tre-projecting a derived ts envelope **silently drops** its C1 rows with no refusal and no\n   601\tdiagnosis. That is cross-track owned and explicitly **not** this round's to fix, so no `src/`\n   602\tfile was touched in FIX-ROUND-2.\n   603\t\n   604\tImplemented as `ts-sp-c1-obligation-test` (`test/yang/typescript_callsite_test.cljc:972-1035`),\n   605\ta documenting regression test that **PASSES by pinning the limitation** and names the\n   606\tobligation in every message, so the suite is not red-lit and a future fix in the shared encoder\n   607\tflips it loudly rather than passing quietly. Part 1 pins the partial-correspondence shape\n   608\t(correspondence present; surviving entries mapped and origin-bearing inside `:input`; injected\n   609\tentries present and origin-less; claimed paths a **strict subset** of the derived tree's\n   610\tapplication occurrences). Part 2 pins the row-drop at re-projection: rows present on entry,\n   611\t`(:yin/frontend-metadata reprojected)` **`[]`** after, canonical `:yin/batch` untouched\n   612\t(provenance-only drop), and the correspondence record still retrievable because\n   613\t`project-envelope` assocs onto the incoming envelope.\n   614\t\n   615\t**J4 — The C1 validator lives in `yang.clojure`, so a ts test depends on the clojure frontend.**\n   616\t`yang/core/uast.cljc:126-128` states: *\"The walk lives here, beside the other shared tree\n   617\ttransforms, so every frontend's module trees finalize the same way; **`yang.clojure` owns the\n   618\tcontract's validation over canonical rows**.\"* I therefore call\n   619\t`yang/validate-call-site-coverage!` and `yang/validate-ast-call-sites!` rather than\n   620\treimplementing them — reimplementing would be precisely the \"parallel mechanism\" the brief\n   945\t## FIX-ROUND-2\n   946\t\n   947\tStep-7 verdict was **FIX-LIST**. The stage-envelope repair (FIX-ROUND-1 item 2) was ACCEPTED\n   948\twith its citation verified (design `:65` / `:133` / `:278` for production-path finalization,\n   949\tplus `:38-39` for origin ownership). Three in-scope items were assigned; all three are done.\n   950\t\n   951\t**Scope discipline observed:** `src/` was **not** touched at all this round. The P1 sol found is\n   952\tin the SHARED encoder (`yin/vm/encoder.cljc:115` and `:158`), owned cross-track, and is\n   953\ttherefore documented and pinned rather than fixed (§7 J3, item 3b below). Both changed files\n   954\tare test-side: `test/yang/typescript_callsite_test.cljc` (grew 760 → **1035** lines, 11 → **14**\n   955\tdeftests) and this findings document. No tests were run (still no shell — `tool_search`\n   956\tre-checked); no commit was made.\n   957\t\n   958\t### Item (1) — P2 coordinate pin + the design-`:139` distinct-admission-token NEGATIVE fixture\n   959\t\n   960\t**`test/yang/typescript_callsite_test.cljc:121-132` (new `project-under`)** — `project` with an\n   961\texplicit batch token, so one packet can be admitted twice under two supplied tokens. This is\n   962\tthe only way to exercise admission freshness from inside a frontend suite.\n   963\t\n   964\t**`:192-195` (new `row-origins`)** — the distinct occurrence origins a C1 relation carries.\n   965\tPlaced *after* `marker-rows` (`:187`) deliberately: I first wrote it above its dependency and\n   966\tcaught the forward reference before saving, since a `defn-` body resolving a later var is a\n   967\tcompile error, not a warning.\n   968\t\n   969\t**`:818-846` (new `reviewed-var-x-paths`)** — the literal expected coordinate paths for the\n   970\tsmallest fixture, `(lower/lower-module-body var-x)` = `var x = 1;`. **Hand-derived, not read\n   971\tback** from `vm/application-occurrences`, from the row grammar (`yin/vm.cljc:1017-1038`:\n   972\t`:application` slots are `[:operator :node]` at position 2, `[:operands :nodes]` at 3,\n   973\t`[:tail? :bool]` at 4; `:lambda` is `[:params :syms]` at 2, `[:body :node]` at 3) and the\n   974\tdesign's path rule at `:39` (*\"scalar child slot steps are row positions; a child in a `nodes`\n   975\tslot is `[slot-position member-index]`. The root is `[]`\"*). The tree is the checked-in golden\n   976\tat `lower_portable_test.cljc:206-217`, so the derivation is reviewable against a literal:\n   977\t\n   978\t```\n   979\t[]                     the cell-binding application (root)\n   980\t[[3 0]]                its operand 0 — (cell/new :js/undefined)\n   981\t[2 3]                  the binding lambda's body — the outer `then`\n   982\t[2 3 [3 0]]            that `then`'s operand 0 — the inner `then`\n   983\t[2 3 [3 0] [3 0]]      the inner `then`'s operand 0 — (cell/set! …)\n   984\t```\n   985\t\n   986\tFive applications, five paths. The docstring also records what must **not** appear: the lambdas\n   987\tat `[2]`, `[2 3 2]`, `[2 3 [3 0] 2]`, and every literal. This is the reference suite's style at\n   988\t`clojure_callsite_test.cljc:119` — literal paths written out, `(:root tree)` substituted for\n   989\tthe address — applied to a ts fixture.\n   990\t\n   991\t**`:849-859` (new `reviewed-var-x-root`)** — the reviewed root pin, **currently `nil`, and I am\n   992\tflagging that explicitly rather than letting it pass silently.** A root is\n   993\t`segment/blake3-<hex>` over the root row; it cannot be derived by hand and I have no shell, so\n   994\tI cannot freeze it honestly. Inventing a plausible-looking hex string would be exactly the\n   995\tfabrication this round's honesty clause forbids. While nil, `coordinate-pin-test` pins the root\n   996\t**relationally** with three host-independent assertions that need no hash; filling the slot\n   997\tfrom the first green run upgrades the pin to a frozen absolute golden with no other edit.\n   998\t**Action for whoever runs this first:** set `reviewed-var-x-root` to\n   999\t`(:root (:tree (project (lower/lower-module-body var-x))))`.\n  1000\t\n  1001\t**`:862-895` (new `coordinate-pin-test`)** — two legs. Leg 1 asserts the hand-derived set\n  1002\t*is* the whole marker set, that it equals the eligible set the canonical rows imply (so hand\n  1003\tderivation and occurrence relation agree), that the origin-free path projection equals the\n  1004\tliteral set, and that coverage validates. Leg 2 is the root pin: the `if-let` takes the frozen\n  1005\tbranch when `reviewed-var-x-root` is set, and until then asserts the root is a well-formed\n  1006\t`segment/blake3-` address, is **identical with the markers stripped**, and is **identical after\n  1007\ta second finalization walk**.\n  1008\t\n  1009\t**`:898-946` (new `distinct-admission-token-test`)** — the design-`:139` / `:272` negative\n  1010\tfixture, in two legs:\n  1011\t\n  1012\t- *Distinct tokens → distinct origins.* One packet (`var-x`), two supplied batch tokens\n  1013\t  `[:hand 0]` / `[:hand 1]`. Asserts the two origins differ; each relation carries exactly its\n  1014\t  own single origin; the two coordinate **sets** differ; **but** the two canonical roots are\n  1015\t  equal and the two origin-free path sets are equal — *\"an occurrence is (content address,\n  1016\t  origin), never content alone\"* and *\"cross-host equality is REPLAY equality, and must not\n  1017\t  turn occurrences into content identities\"* (design `:272`). Coverage validates under each\n  1018\t  admission separately.\n  1019\t- *Retry keeps its token.* The same packet admitted twice under the SAME token reproduces the\n  1020\t  SAME origin and the SAME coordinate set — the other half of design `:139` (*\"C1 requires fresh\n  1021\t  admission identity **while retry keeps the token**\"*), pinning that a retry fabricates no new\n  1022\t  admission identity.\n  1023\t\n  1024\tThe first leg also asserts `(= [:hand 0] (:yang.cst/unit var-x))` — both admissions carry the\n  1025\t**same packet identity**, which is precisely why the supplied token must carry freshness:\n  1026\tdesign `:139`'s *\"packet identity alone is insufficient if reused for distinct admissions.\"*\n  1027\t\n  1028\t### Item (2) — P2 closed-set audit extended to `jssp`, plus a renamed-wrapper negative fixture\n  1029\t\n  1030\t**`:709-760` (new shared helpers).** The audit was refactored out of the deftest body so the\n  1031\treal scan and the negative fixture run the *same* predicate — otherwise the fixture would prove\n  1032\tnothing about the production audit:\n  1033\t\n  1034\t- `guest-surface-deny-strings` `:709-713` — the deny-list, now a named def.\n  1035\t- `exposed-name?` `:715-718` — the name-based leg as a reusable predicate.\n  1036\t- `exception-track-doors` `:721-729` — `#{cell/dsg__new-provenance cell/dsg__provenance\n  1037\t  dsg__condition-exception}`, documented as the DIFFERENT key family (landed `8c10c5b7`) and as\n  1061\t\n  1062\t### Item (3) — findings amendments\n  1063\t\n  1064\t**(3a) Golden-repair claim corrected — §1.2.** My FIX-ROUND-0 text said the goldens were\n  1065\trepaired by finalizing the expected side, implying all six. **Inaccurate: five of six do; the\n  1066\tsixth, `linked_prelude_test.cljc:62`, strips `:yang/call-site` from the PRODUCED side**\n  1067\t(`(dissoc (last operands) :tail? :yang/call-site)`) because that assertion's purpose is to\n  1068\tcompare one operand of the published `jssp` module tree against a plain `sexp->uast` rendering.\n  1069\tsol verified precision is preserved; the choice stands and only my description was wrong. §1.2\n  1070\tnow says so explicitly, names which five finalize and which one strips, and records why.\n  1071\t\n  1072\t**(3b) J3 replaced by the ruled obligation `TS-SP-C1` — §7.** My FIX-ROUND-1 J3 asked the\n  1073\torchestrator to *rule* on whether the ts safepoint path should supply an admission origin, and\n  1074\tframed the derived table as merely \"not a complete-coverage claim\". Per sol's ruling that\n  1075\tframing is replaced: omitting `:injected-origin` is **named obligation `TS-SP-C1`** — a ts\n  1076\tderived envelope carries **partial correspondence**, which is explicitly *not* complete\n  1077\tderived-tree adoption — and **derived-envelope re-projection stays OUTSIDE the accepted C1\n  1078\troutes until the shared-encoder gap is resolved.** The gap is P1, lives in the SHARED encoder at\n  1079\t`encoder.cljc:115` (the already-projected member arm reads `:frontend-metadata` off the member,\n  1080\twhere `derive-envelope` never puts it — the rows ride the envelope) and `:158`\n  1081\t(`project-envelope`'s `mapcat` overwrites the envelope-level relation with the empty\n  1082\tconcatenation), so re-projection **silently drops** the C1 rows with no refusal and no\n  1083\tdiagnosis. Cross-track owned; not touched.\n  1084\t\n  1085\tNew documenting test: **`ts-sp-c1-obligation-test` `:972-1035`**, with `derived-medium`\n  1086\t`:954-957` and the `derive` helper `:960-969` (which calls `safepoint/derive-envelope` at its\n  1087\t3-arity, exactly as all four ts callers do — `safepoint/transform` at `yang/safepoint.cljc:455`,\n  1088\t`e2e_test.clj:71-86`, `safepoint_test.clj:132-136`, `linked_prelude_test.cljc:249-262`). **It\n  1089\tPASSES by documenting the limitation and names `TS-SP-C1` in every message; the suite is not\n  1090\tred-lit.** Part 1 pins the partial-correspondence shape: correspondence present; surviving\n  1091\tentries mapped and origin-bearing inside `:input`; injected entries present and\n  1092\t`(not-any? :origin injected)`; every claimed path a real application of the derived tree\n  1093\t`(every? claimed-paths eligible-paths)`; and the claim a **STRICT** subset\n  1094\t`(not= claimed-paths eligible-paths)`. Part 2 pins the row-drop at re-projection: rows present\n  1095\ton entry, `(is (= [] (:yin/frontend-metadata reprojected)))` after, canonical `:yin/batch`\n  1096\tidentical (provenance-only drop), and `:yang.safepoint/correspondence` still retrievable\n  1097\tbecause `project-envelope` assocs onto the incoming envelope. §4.4 still describes the\n  1098\tbehaviour; §7 J3 now carries the ruling.\n  1099\t\n  1100\t**(3c) Admission-freshness obligation, recorded.** From sol's ask-1 qualification, as relayed to\n  1101\tme, the standing obligation is: **admission freshness is a property of the SUPPLIED batch token,\n  1102\tnever of the packet.** One packet admitted twice as two distinct admissions must yield two\n  1103\tdistinct occurrence origins; a retry of one admission keeps its token and therefore keeps its\n  1104\torigin. Packet identity alone is insufficient when reused for distinct admissions — design\n  1105\t`:139`, verbatim: *\"It currently supplies medium plus packet unit as batch token. Audit distinct\n  1106\tre-admissions of the same unit: C1 requires fresh admission identity while retry keeps the\n  1107\ttoken; packet identity alone is insufficient if reused for distinct admissions.\"* Reinforced by\n  1108\t`:272`: *\"A separate negative fixture admits identical text twice with distinct supplied\n  1109\tadmission tokens and demands different origins. Cross-host equality is replay equality; it must\n  1110\tnot turn occurrences into content identities.\"*\n  1111\t\n  1112\tFor ts concretely: `core/stage.cljc:36-38` derives the batch token from the packet's\n  1113\t`:yang.cst/unit`, and every fixture in this suite is built by `lower-portable-test/packet`, which\n  1114\thardcodes `:yang.cst/unit [:hand 0]`. So **a ts host that re-admits the same unit for a genuinely\n  1115\tdistinct admission must mint a fresh token at the composition boundary** — the frontend will not\n  1116\tdo it for them, and must not. `distinct-admission-token-test` `:898-946` is the pin, and it\n  1117\tasserts the shared-packet-identity premise explicitly so the obligation cannot be satisfied by\n  1118\taccident. **Caveat, stated honestly:** I do not have sol's ask-1 text verbatim — only the\n  1119\torchestrator's one-line summary — so this paragraph records the obligation as I derived it from\n  1120\tthat summary plus design `:139`/`:272`. If ask-1 said more (a specific ts host, a specific\n  1121\tre-admission path, or a numbering rule for tokens), it is not captured here and should be\n  1122\trelayed so I can pin it too.\n  1123\t\n  1124\t### Process note — one more off-by-one caught by the regex check, not by reading\n  1125\t\n  1126\tRewriting `export-audit-pin-test` introduced a fourth closing paren on the\n  1127\t`\"…scans form trees\"` line (`:797`), which closed the `deftest` early and would have left the\n  1128\tfinal `(testing …)` block top-level with an unmatched delimiter — the *same* defect class as\n  1129\tFIX-ROUND-1's `linked_prelude_test.cljc:66` compile error. The exact-count regex sweep caught it\n  1130\tbefore handoff (`scans form trees\"\\){3}$` did not match; reading the line did not reveal it).\n  1131\tThis is the second time in two rounds that the regex check found what reading missed, so the\n  1132\tFIX-ROUND-1 rule is confirmed worth keeping: **every closing line in every touched file gets an\n  1133\texact-count regex assertion, and any edit that changes a line count inside a nested form gets\n  1134\tits net paren delta computed against the pristine text.** Verified this round at `:772` (4),\n  1135\t`:783` (4), `:789` (1), `:792` (1), `:797` (3), `:810` (3), `:879` (5), `:895` (6), `:931` (5),\n  1136\t`:945` (4), `:969` (4), `:1008` (3), `:1034` (4). All 14 `deftest` forms and every helper `defn-`\n  1137\tconfirmed at column 0 in dependency order, with no forward references.\n  1138\t\n  1139\t### Still outstanding after FIX-ROUND-2\n  1140\t\n  1141\t- **`reviewed-var-x-root` is `nil`** — the absolute root golden is unfrozen; see item (1) for\n  1142\t  the one-line action that freezes it.\n  1143\t- The three new deftests (`coordinate-pin-test`, `distinct-admission-token-test`,\n  1144\t  `ts-sp-c1-obligation-test`) and the rewritten `export-audit-pin-test` have **never been\n  1145\t  executed**. Highest risk in `ts-sp-c1-obligation-test`: it assumes `hooks/profile` injects at\n  1146\t  least one hook into `(lower/lower-packet while-x {:prelude :linked})` — true by construction\n  1147\t  (`while-x` carries a `:loop` mark and `hooks/profile` maps `:loop` → `js.sp/loop`), and\n  1148\t  `(is (seq injected))` fails informatively if that ever stops holding. Second highest:\n  1149\t  `coordinate-pin-test`'s hand-derived path set, which is exactly the kind of thing a first run\n  1150\t  should be expected to correct.\n  1151\t- `linked_prelude_test` still needs its re-run (FIX-ROUND-1's outstanding item), as do\n  1152\t  `float-address-test` (5/20) and `prelude-module-test`.\n  1153\t- No commit. Working tree: 6 modified files + `test/yang/typescript_callsite_test.cljc`, with\n  1154\t  `.qwen/` and this findings document excluded from the intended commit set.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
